From aca3d608f87b552bf58474c9243a5819cc21642a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 16:21:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] kanban resume: a core-update requeue does not spend
 the operator resume receipt

Problem: an operator resume grants an active_pr-held card one continuation. When
hermes-sync's restart-safe core-update requeue reclaimed that continuation run, the
guard held the card active_pr again with no work done (MacBook board,
t_01a1d560c6d3bc3c, 2026-11-13: run 14054 requeued, a second resume was needed).

Why: resumed_after() compared the receipt's after_run with the raw newest task_runs
row, so the requeued run counted as the run that consumed it. Every other one-shot
handoff (released_after, the dependency-park handoff) already passes over requeues.

Fix: walk runs newest first, passing over core-update requeues and attested unused
grants (kanban_core_update_requeue._skipped, the view latest_run_skipping_requeues
uses). The receipt holds while the named run is reached before any real run, so it
survives a requeue after it and is honoured when it names a requeued run itself. Fail
closed as before: the next real claim, a crash/timeout/gave_up/spawn_failed event, or
a reclaim with any other reason spends it.

Proof: tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_operator_resume_requeue.py. RED on b3745e62d5
(2 failed: guard returned active_pr after the requeue); GREEN with the fix, plus all
14 test files importing kanban_operator_resume / kanban_hold_release /
kanban_core_update_requeue (230 passed).
